{"id": "47:3", "fails": ["native", "sayable"], "remarks": [{"words": "Com a exultação de toda a terra é fundado o monte Sião", "problem": "The prepositional phrase is fronted and the subject comes last, after a passive verb. The ear has to hold 'com a exultação de toda a terra' in suspense before it learns what is being founded. That word order is Latin, not Portuguese.", "alternative": "O monte Sião é fundado com a exultação de toda a terra, * os lados do norte, a cidade do grande Rei."}]},
{"id": "47:4", "fails": ["sayable"], "remarks": [{"words": "quando ele a amparar", "problem": "'ele a am-' runs three vowels together and clogs a very short colon. The Latin names no subject, and the subject is already clear from 'Deus' in the first half.", "alternative": "quando a amparar."}]},
{"id": "47:5", "fails": ["native", "plain"], "remarks": [{"words": "Porque eis que", "problem": "'Porque eis que' stacks two connectives and sounds heavy at the head of the verse. 'Pois' says the same with a lighter onset.", "alternative": "Pois eis que os reis da terra se congregaram"}, {"words": "reuniram-se juntos", "problem": "This sounds like the schoolroom pleonasm 'reunir juntos'. 'Convenerunt' means 'came together', and the plain verb avoids the redundancy.", "alternative": "vieram juntos."}]},
{"id": "47:6", "fails": ["sayable", "native"], "remarks": [{"words": "Eles, ao verem, assim se admiraram, foram perturbados, foram abalados", "problem": "The first colon is too long for one breath. The two analytic passives, 'foram perturbados, foram abalados', add syllables and weaken the three-beat Latin run ('admiráti sunt, conturbáti sunt, commóti sunt'). Three parallel reflexive verbs keep the triple and shorten the colon.", "alternative": "Eles, vendo, assim se admiraram, perturbaram-se, abalaram-se:"}, {"words": "o tremor apoderou-se deles", "problem": "In Brazilian speech the clitic comes before the verb here. The enclitic form sounds like European or bookish Portuguese.", "alternative": "o tremor se apoderou deles."}]},
{"id": "47:10", "fails": ["native"], "remarks": [{"words": "Acolhemos, Deus,", "problem": "A bare 'Deus' as a vocative in the middle of a sentence sounds abrupt in Portuguese. Liturgical Portuguese uses 'ó Deus', which also gives the colon a better rhythm.", "alternative": "Acolhemos, ó Deus, a vossa misericórdia, * no meio do vosso templo."}]},
{"id": "47:11", "fails": ["native", "sayable"], "remarks": [{"words": "Como o vosso nome, Deus, assim também o vosso louvor, até os confins da terra", "problem": "The same bare vocative 'Deus' appears, and the comma before 'até' strands the phrase so it seems to hang without a verb. If the comma is dropped, 'até os confins' reads as reaching out with 'o vosso louvor'.", "alternative": "Como o vosso nome, ó Deus, assim também o vosso louvor até os confins da terra:"}]}
],
"overall": "The Portuguese is sober and faithful and mostly prays well. The hymnic verses (2, 9, 12, 15) have real weight. Where it stumbles, it follows Latin word order or analytic passives too closely (3, 6), or uses small bookish habits: enclisis, a bare vocative 'Deus', and 'reuniram-se juntos'. Each of these can be fixed without touching the Latin's images.", "worstLine": "47:6", "bestLine": "47:9"}
